# TEMPORARY, together with the ci-testing entry in build-on-push.yml.
#
# One "<kernel> <url>" per line. Each named kernel is tested against the build
# at that URL, for every suite, and nothing is rebuilt. The kernel has to be one
# of the keys of .github/lava-boards.yml, which is what decides the boards:
#
#   qcom-next   https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/35226840721-1/
#   linux-next  https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/35100000000-1/
#
# Leave it with no entry, as committed, and the qcom-next images are built and
# tested as they are on main.
#
# A URL is the artifact prefix of a previous run, which holds the images of
# every suite. Take it from the "Downloads URL" line of that run's debos job, or
# from the build-url metadata of any LAVA job it submitted.
#
# Why one URL per kernel rather than a kernel axis on the build job: debos
# uploads to <run id>-<attempt>/ and names the images after the suite alone, so
# two kernels built in one run would write the same file over each other. Each
# kernel gets its own build only by being its own workflow run, which on main is
# what the daily workflows are.
